\cf0 \strokec2 Chanta had discovered that there were few things more frustrating than trying to reach someone who had expressly asked him not to get into trouble.\
From the lawns north of the carpark he could see the signs for the Traverse. Beyond them was a broad ramp descending beneath the office building, lined with shops and displays advertising the sort of luxuries people could generally afford only after their ancestors had been stealing things for several generations. It looked like a particularly expensive shopping arcade that had run out of street and decided to continue underground.\
The High Elvis had made discreet enquiries. The ramp led to a station, and the station led to the enormous underground establishment where Eloise conducted some of her more secretive business.\
Chanta wanted to go straight down there.\
The High Elvis persuaded him to wait.\
The problem was not the Shakti guards, although there were plenty of those. It was the civilians.\
The compound was having a Christmas party of extraordinary proportions, much of it entirely unscheduled. People who had come to watch Candid Criminal were discovering that being locked inside the grounds of one of the country's richest women was a considerable improvement on being locked outside them. There were offices with views, empty townhouses, a lookout, enough food to provision an invading army, and more alcohol than the army could reasonably have conquered.\
Some Shakti employees had begun taking visitors on private tours. Others had organised afterparties, invitations to which had acquired considerable value despite being distributed by people who had no authority to invite anybody anywhere.\
Most of these festivities remained above ground. Unfortunately, a few employees had access to the Traverse and had discovered that mentioning a secret underground establishment could achieve things that talking about their salaries and expensive shoes could not.\
Chanta watched the occasional little party descend the ramp, sometimes returning considerably later with different people, fewer clothes, or more emphatic opinions about the evening.\
One young woman emerged loudly explaining that she had been taken to a conference room with a breathtaking view of the forest. Her escort was trying to persuade her that she hadn't.\
There were long periods when nobody went near the station.\
Each time, Chanta prepared to move. Each time, somebody else appeared.\
The Jones clones had stationed themselves at observation points around the carpark and had begun keeping records of everybody entering and leaving. They were taking particular interest in discrepancies between the numbers going down and those returning, and had already developed several theories about what Shakti Corp was doing with the missing persons.\
The Elvisettes had found somewhere comfortable among the encamped Elvi. Some slept. Others participated in the festivities, which occasionally required them to explain that they were not prepared to become anyone's Christmas present without being consulted first.\
Chanta did not sleep. He sat in a borrowed camping chair with his device in his hand, occasionally checking whether Sarah had called.\
The High Elvis visited him several times.\
\'93We can take the station,\'94 he said.\
\'93That's not the problem.\'94\
\'93I understand.\'94\
\'93What if we hurt somebody?\'94\
\'93We shall endeavour not to.\'94\
\'93That's a pretty weak promise.\'94\
\'93It is the strongest one available.\'94\
Chanta didn't like that.\
Neither did he like the observation that somebody might remain underground indefinitely. He could wait for the last passenger to leave the station, but he couldn't know whether there was still an accountant asleep on a conference table or a couple conducting an unusually thorough inspection of a storage cupboard.\
Eventually, the High Elvis suggested that they would have to accept some risk.\
Chanta regarded him with considerable disappointment. Risk was something other people took, generally after Chanta had spent considerable time advising them not to.\
\'93It's Sarah,\'94 he said.\
\'93I know.\'94\
\'93And Little Clinton.\'94\
\'93I know.\'94\
Chanta returned his attention to the station.\
\'93Okay,\'94 he said eventually. \'93But nobody gets hurt if we can help it.\'94\
The High Elvis went to summon the others.\
They had barely begun gathering when a handful of Shakti employees appeared from the direction of the offices and made their way down the ramp. They carried equipment cases and had the unhappy appearance of people whose employers had scheduled an important experiment on Christmas morning.\
Chanta swore.\
The Jones clones reported that the employees had passed through the station's security checks and boarded a Traverse car. Nobody else had followed.\
Chanta waited for the car to depart.\
Then he got up.\
