\cf0 \strokec2 The station concourse was wider than Chanta expected. The ramp had descended past shops and recessed doorways, opening onto a well-lit space that had been designed to accommodate the entire workforce of a considerable office building without requiring anyone to touch anybody else.\
Beyond the concourse were the platforms.\
The armed security contingent had apparently spent most of the night supervising people who had no legitimate reason to be there, and were in no mood for another Christmas surprise.\
They received one anyway.\
The Jones clones appeared from the ramp with their pistols already drawn. Within seconds they had spread across the concourse and taken aim at the guards.\
There were more guards than Joneses, but not overwhelmingly more. The guards had automatic weapons, some of which were already in their hands. None of them seemed inclined to discover whether they could bring those weapons to bear before the Joneses fired.\
A Jones with a blue tie stepped forward.\
\'93You will release Sarah Jacobson.\'94\
The senior guard glanced at the armed men confronting him. He had encountered enough security problems during the night to know that none of them had been adequately covered during his training.\
\'93Who?\'94\
\'93Sarah Jacobson.\'94\
\'93Don't know her.\'94\
\'93You will release her.\'94\
\'93Look, mate. I don't know who she is.\'94\
\'93Sarah Jacobson.\'94\
\'93Repeating it isn't helping.\'94\
Another guard ventured that their automatic weapons considerably outgunned the pistols aimed at them.\
Nobody seemed particularly comforted by the observation.\
Chanta pushed forward between two Joneses.\
\'93Hey. Guys. Come on. Put your guns away.\'94\
Neither side complied.\
\'93I just need to see Sarah,\'94 he said to the guards. \'93She's having a baby. Well, not right this minute, I hope, but pretty soon. I'm not here to hurt anybody.\'94\
The guards were less impressed by this assurance than Chanta had expected, given that the men accompanying him looked capable of invading several moderately sized countries before breakfast.\
\'93Look, I'm harmless,\'94 Chanta continued. \'93Really. Let me pass and you guys can all play cards till I get back.\'94\
One guard raised his rifle and aimed it at Chanta.\
A Jones fired.\
The report cracked around the concourse. Everybody flinched. Several guards tightened their grips on their weapons. Nobody fired back.\
There was no blood. Nobody fell.\
The guard who had been aiming at Chanta stared at himself.\
\'93Missed me,\'94 he announced.\
\'93You will check your cigarettes,\'94 said the Jones.\
\'93What?\'94\
\'93Your cigarettes. You will inspect them.\'94\
The guard hesitated, then gave a nervous laugh. He lowered his rifle sufficiently to retrieve the packet from the cargo pocket on his thigh.\
He opened it.\
The second cigarette from the right in the front row had a neat hole through it.\
So did the third from the right in the back row.\
He looked at the Jones.\
The Jones had not lowered his pistol.\
\'93You will release Sarah Jacobson,\'94 he said, \'93and you will stop smoking. It's bad for you.\'94\
The guard looked again at his cigarettes. Years of diligent work by the primary-school teachers' tobacco cartel had been undone by a single gunshot.\
Nobody moved.\
Then, from the arcade to one side of the concourse, came singing.\
\'93Glory, glory Holy Momma!\'94\
The Elvi emerged in a procession, the High Elvis among them, with guitar cases, capes, and the Elvisettes adding harmonies to the growing chorus.\
\'93Glory, glory Holy Momma!\'94\
Chanta turned towards them.\
\'93Oh, tax,\'94 he said.\
The effect on the Jones clones was immediate.\
One lowered his pistol and began to weep.\
Another made a peculiar strangled noise and covered his mouth. A third dropped to his knees, shoulders shaking. Within moments, the entire contingent was overcome.\
The sight of so many large, identical, immaculately dressed men dissolving into tears was unsettling enough. The noise they made was worse.\
Several guards forgot their weapons entirely.\
One approached a sobbing Jones and put a hand on his shoulder.\
\'93Hey, mate. It's all right.\'94\
The Jones buried his face against the guard's chest.\
Another guard knelt beside a clone who had begun pounding the floor with his fist.\
\'93Whatever it is, you're going to be okay.\'94\
A Jones clutched at a guard's sleeve and wept into it.\
The Elvi stopped singing.\
The Joneses recovered.\
The guard comforting the first Jones discovered that the man had suddenly acquired sufficient composure to break his grip, twist his arm behind his back and put him face down on the concourse.\
The other guards fared little better.\
The Joneses moved with astonishing speed, extracting weapons from hands and depositing their owners on the floor. The guards who had retained enough suspicion to keep their distance attempted to retreat.\
The Elvi tossed Gyges discs.\
The first sent two guards flying backwards into a wall. Another overturned a security desk, scattering its occupants and everything they had been using to conduct their investigation of the evening's more intimate adventures.\
A third disc produced a violent sideways displacement of several guards who had been trying to reach the platform.\
One landed in a display of Christmas merchandise. Another struck the floor hard enough to lose consciousness.\
Chanta shouted for everybody to stop.\
By then, there wasn't much opposition left.\
The Jones clones began binding the guards, working through those they had subdued personally and the others who had been knocked senseless. Their wounded pride appeared to have been entirely restored by the opportunity to restrain other people.\
The High Elvis approached Chanta.\
\'93The station is ours, Great Teacher.\'94\
Chanta surveyed the concourse.\
\'93Well, nobody's dead.\'94\
\'93An admirable result.\'94\
\'93Why did the Dick Tracys start crying?\'94\
\'93I do not know.\'94\
Chanta looked at the clones. Several were now inspecting their prisoners' clothing and possessions with the professional enthusiasm of men who suspected that the entire incident had been arranged to conceal a much larger conspiracy.\
\'93Bloody weird,\'94 he said.\
Then the Elvi began tossing discs at the Traverse cars.\
A passenger car lurched sideways. Its underside struck the platform edge with a noise that drove Chanta's hands to his ears. Another disc sent the car bucking against the maglev guideway.\
The next car tipped at an angle that suggested it had no immediate intention of ever carrying passengers again.\
Metal tore. Glass burst. Something substantial beneath the platform cracked, and a trembling ran through the concourse floor.\
\'93HEY!\'94\
The Elvi continued until the platform had acquired the appearance of a transport disaster that had been thoroughly investigated by a committee of demolition experts.\
Chanta rounded on the High Elvis.\
\'93What the tax are you doing?\'94\
\'93Disabling the Traverse.\'94\
\'93I can see that! How are we supposed to get to Sarah?\'94\
\'93By the service road.\'94\
Chanta stared at him.\
\'93The trucks, Great Teacher.\'94\
\'93I know about the trucks!\'94\
\'93It was in the plan.\'94\
\'93Well, I didn't think you were going to wreck the whole bloody train!\'94\
\'93We have not wrecked the whole train.\'94\
Chanta looked at the platform.\
\'93Not for lack of trying.\'94\
The High Elvis seemed satisfied with the distinction.\
Behind them, the Joneses had completed binding the conscious guards and were inspecting the injuries of the others. One had confiscated the cigarette packet and was preparing what appeared to be an evidence report.\
A security access door at the end of the concourse led into the station's service levels. The captured guards supplied the means to open it.\
Beyond was a vehicle bay, with Shakti service trucks standing beside a roadway that descended towards Ruined Castle. The road ran alongside the Traverse, separated from the passenger system by substantial structural barriers.\
The Elvi collected their discs and guitar cases.\
The Jones clones arranged for their prisoners to be secured away from the damaged platform, then took possession of the trucks.\
Chanta watched the Elvisettes climb aboard with the Elvi. The High Elvis joined him in the leading vehicle. The Joneses distributed themselves through the convoy, keeping their pistols and their suspicions to hand.\
Chanta looked back towards the concourse.\
\'93Are those guards going to be all right?\'94\
\'93They have been disarmed and restrained,\'94 the High Elvis said.\
\'93That's not what I asked.\'94\
\'93No one has been killed.\'94\
\'93Yeah, well. Let's keep it that way.\'94\
The trucks started down the service road.\
Behind them, the Traverse was ruined.\
Ahead lay Sarah.\
